<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boston</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boston is a city in the northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, capital of Massachusetts. Its museums hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-carpet">Persian carpets</a> from the classical period of Safavid weaving. The site mentions it, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, as a home of the finest carpets produced in the royal workshops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, a legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>'s artistic patronage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boston.jpg" alt="Boston" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Howard · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISH_WC_Boston4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Boston stands on the Shawmut Peninsula, beside Boston Harbor, an arm of Massachusetts Bay. Its highest point is Bellevue Hill, at 330 feet above sea level. Since independence the city has expanded well beyond the original peninsula by filling in land and annexing neighbouring towns. Boston Common, near Beacon Hill, is part of the Emerald Necklace, a string of parks designed by Frederick Law Olmsted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before European colonisation the surrounding region was inhabited by the Massachusett people, who built small seasonal communities there. When settlers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a> arrived in 1630 they found the Shawmut Peninsula nearly empty of Native people, most of whom had died of diseases brought by earlier settlers and traders.</p><p>The first European to live there was William Blaxton, an Anglican cleric educated at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He invited Isaac Johnson, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a>, to cross from the failing colony of Charlestown and share the peninsula, and in September 1630 the Puritans made the crossing. Johnson, in one of his last acts before his death on 30 September 1630, named the settlement after his hometown of Boston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>. Puritan ethics and their focus on education shaped the early city, and Boston Latin School, the first public school in America, was founded in 1635.</p><p>Boston was home to several seminal events of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>: the Boston Massacre of 1770, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> of 1773, the Battle of Bunker Hill in 1775 and the Siege of Boston from 1775 to 1776. After independence it became an important port and manufacturing centre as well as a centre of education and culture, and it has been called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of America for its literary culture. Its firsts include Boston Common in 1634, the oldest public park in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the Tremont Street subway of 1897, the first subway system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Boston is the capital and most populous city of Massachusetts and a cultural and financial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. The Boston Red Sox play at Fenway Park, built in 1912, which is the oldest stadium in active use among the four major American professional sports leagues.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boston” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It has spread to the Dominican Republic, Puerto Rico, France and Canada, and major temples stand in Miami, Oakland, <b>Boston</b>, New York City and Washington, D.C.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;John Brown's Body&quot; was publicly played, perhaps for the first time, at a flag-raising at Fort Warren near <b>Boston</b> on 12 May 1861, and the battalion soon prepared the words for publication with the publisher C. S. Hall.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Since then it has been recorded by choirs, early-music ensembles and rock bands alike: the <b>Boston</b> Camerata under Joel Cohen (1985), the Finnish singer Pertti Neumann (1986), the King's Singers (1990), Anúna with Eimear Quinn (1996), the Mediæval Bæbes (1997), the boy choir Libera (2001), Tenebrae in an…</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudete">Gaudete</a></li><li><blockquote>It was first performed the day before, on 15 September 1857, at Ordway Hall in <b>Boston</b>, by the blackface minstrel performer Johnny Pell.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jingle-bells">Jingle Bells</a></li><li><blockquote>The song was played in public, perhaps for the first time, at a flag-raising at Fort Warren near <b>Boston</b> on Sunday 12 May 1861, the month after the Civil War began.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li><li><blockquote>He first published it with these words in his Occasional Psalm and Hymn Tunes (1836) under the name &quot;Antioch&quot;, marked &quot;From Handel&quot;, again in The Modern Psalmist (1839), and in the arrangement now usual in The National Psalmist (<b>Boston</b>, 1848).</blockquote><a href="/songs/joy-to-the-world">Joy to the World</a></li></ul></section>
